```c
ret = fr_value_str_aunescape(ctx, &buff, &FR_SBUFF_IN(in, len), SIZE_MAX, quote);
```

This calls `fr_sbuff_out_bstrncpy` with `in` sbuff having `end_i = in + len`. The `len = data_len - 1`. The `copy` buffer has `data_len` bytes. So reading `len = data_len - 1` bytes from `copy` is within bounds.

But wait - `fr_sbuff_out_bstrncpy` does:
```c
while (fr_sbuff_used_total(&our_in) < len) {
    size_t chunk_len;
    remaining = (len - fr_sbuff_used_total(&our_in));
    if (!fr_sbuff_extend(&our_in)) break;
    chunk_len = fr_sbuff_remaining(&our_in);
    if (chunk_len > remaining) chunk_len = remaining;
    FILL_OR_GOTO_DONE(out, &our_in, chunk_len);
}
```

`fr_sbuff_extend` calls `fr_sbuff_extend_lowat(NULL, &our_in, 1)`. Since `our_in` has no `extend` function (it's a plain `FR_SBUFF_IN`), `fr_sbuff_extend_lowat` returns `remaining` if `remaining >= 1`, or 0 if `remaining == 0`. So the loop copies `min(remaining, len - used)` bytes each iteration. This is safe.

